        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Propane 2-Burner, Butane Single or Backpacking Stove: Which Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Camping stoves come in three families, and the right one depends far more on how you will use it than on any single spec. A stove for a family campsite, a stove for the back patio during a power outage and a stove for a three-day backpacking trip are different tools. Most buyers searching for the best camping stove need one of the first two, and the good news is that the same stove often covers both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane Two-Burner Stoves</h3>

          <p>
            The suitcase-style propane stove is the classic: two burners side by side under a lid that folds up to become a windscreen. It runs on the green 1 lb propane cylinders sold at hardware stores, big-box stores and many gas stations, and most models can also run from a larger tank with a maker-approved adapter hose. Two burners mean you can boil water and fry at the same time, which is what turns camping food into real meals. The Camp Chef Everest 2X and Coleman Triton+ in this guide are this type. They are the best choice for car camping, tailgating and cooking for a family outdoors during an outage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Butane Single-Burner Stoves</h3>

          <p>
            Butane cassette stoves are the flat, briefcase-sized burners you see at hot-pot restaurants and in emergency kits. A slim 8 oz butane canister slides into the side. They are cheap, compact, quiet and easy to light, and a single strong burner like the Iwatani 35FW cooks surprisingly well. Their weakness is cold: butane does not vaporize well near and below freezing, so output fades on winter mornings. They make an excellent second stove, a budget outage stove for mild climates, or a tabletop burner for cooking outdoors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Backpacking Canister Stoves</h3>

          <p>
            Backpacking stoves screw onto threaded isobutane-propane canisters and weigh a few ounces. Some, like the MSR PocketRocket 2, are open burners that hold any pot. Others, like the Jetboil Flash, are integrated systems where the pot locks to the burner, capturing heat so they boil water fast on very little fuel. They are made for one or two people, not for frying bacon for six, but they are also the smallest, lightest stove you can put in a go-bag.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Choose in One Minute</h3>

          <p>
            Cooking for three or more, at a campsite or outside during outages: buy a propane two-burner. Cooking for one or two in mild weather and want low cost: a butane single burner. Hiking, or you want a pocket-sized emergency boiler: a backpacking canister stove. Unsure which fuel you will be able to buy after a storm: the Gas One GS-3900PB runs on either butane or 1 lb propane.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-outage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Can You Use a Camping Stove During a Power Outage? Yes, Outdoors Only
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the most important section on the page. When the power goes out, a camping stove is the easiest way to keep cooking, boiling water and making coffee. But propane and butane camp stoves are outdoor appliances. Every flame that burns fuel makes carbon monoxide, and a camp stove has no flue, no vent hood and no oxygen depletion sensor. In a closed-up house during a winter outage, with windows shut and no exhaust fan running, carbon monoxide can build up to dangerous levels without any smell or warning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Indoors Is Dangerous: Carbon Monoxide</h3>

          <p>
            Carbon monoxide poisoning after storms is common enough that public health agencies warn about it every winter: generators in garages, grills on porches and camp stoves in kitchens are among the usual causes. Early symptoms such as headache, dizziness and nausea are easy to mistake for a cold or exhaustion, and people who are asleep may not notice them at all. Never use a camp stove as a space heater, never run one in a tent, garage or closed room, and never use one with everyone asleep. If you need heat, read our guide to the <a href="/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/">best indoor heater for a power outage</a>, which covers heaters actually rated for indoor use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where to Cook Instead</h3>

          <p>
            Set the stove up outside on a stable, non-flammable surface: a patio, a driveway, a picnic table or a folding table in the yard. Keep it well away from doors, windows and air intakes so fumes do not drift back inside, and away from siding, fences, overhangs and anything that can catch fire. An open carport or a porch with open sides and plenty of air movement is better than an enclosed space, but in the open is best. Bring a headlamp or lantern for after dark; our <a href="/best-battery-powered-lantern/">best battery powered lantern</a> guide has options that run for days.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Butane 'Indoor Use' Label</h3>

          <p>
            Some butane cassette stoves, including the Iwatani 35FW, are sold as indoor and outdoor cooktops. That label refers to normal tabletop cooking in a ventilated room, the way a restaurant or home cook might use one for hot pot. It is not permission to cook in a sealed-up house for days with no ventilation. If a listing says indoor use is allowed, follow the maker's instructions exactly, open windows, run ventilation if you have power for it, and still keep a carbon monoxide alarm working nearby. If the listing says outdoor use only, as many do, believe it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep a Battery CO Alarm Working</h3>

          <p>
            A plug-in carbon monoxide alarm without battery backup is useless in an outage. Make sure you have a battery or battery-backup CO alarm on each level of the home and near sleeping areas, and test it before storm season. Our <a href="/best-carbon-monoxide-detector/">best carbon monoxide detector</a> guide compares battery, plug-in with backup and combination models.
          </p>

