        <div id="btu" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Many BTUs Does a Camping Stove Need? Less Than the Box Suggests
          </h2>

          <p>
            BTU is the number printed biggest on the box, so it is the number most people shop by. It measures how much fuel a burner can burn per hour at full output, not how well it cooks. A high BTU rating helps in cold weather and with big pots, but wind protection, burner design and how well the pot captures heat matter just as much.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Number Actually Tells You</h3>

          <p>
            A stove listed at 22,000 BTU total, like the Coleman Triton+, usually means two burners of about 11,000 each. The Camp Chef Everest 2X lists two 20,000 BTU burners. Butane single burners range from about 7,650 BTU on the Coleman Classic to 15,000 on the Iwatani 35FW and Gas One GS-3900PB. Backpacking stoves are usually rated by boil time instead, because that is what their buyers care about.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Around 10,000 BTU Per Burner Covers Most Cooking</h3>

          <p>
            For frying eggs, simmering a stew or boiling a pot of pasta water for two to four people, a burner around 10,000 BTU is enough. You will wait a little longer for a big pot to boil, but meals cook fine. More output mostly helps when you cook for a crowd, use large pots, cook in cold air or face steady wind.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">More BTU Burns More Fuel</h3>

          <p>
            Higher output on full blast empties a cylinder faster. That is not a reason to avoid a strong stove, since you rarely run it at full, but it is a reason to plan fuel by burn time rather than by how big the BTU number looks. For outage planning, a stove with a stated burn time per cylinder is easier to stock for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Propane vs Butane vs Isobutane: Fuel Availability Decides
          </h2>

          <p>
            The fuel you can actually buy, store and use in your climate is a bigger decision than which brand of stove you pick. Each of the three common fuels has a clear strength and a clear weakness.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1 lb Propane Cylinders</h3>

          <p>
            The green 16.4 oz propane cylinder is the most widely available camp fuel in North America. Hardware stores, big-box stores, sporting goods stores and many gas stations sell it. Propane works in cold weather far better than butane, which is why it is the default for two-burner camp stoves. The downside is waste: the small cylinders are bulky, and empties need proper disposal or recycling according to local rules.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">8 oz Butane Canisters</h3>

          <p>
            Butane canisters are cheap, slim and easy to stack in a closet, and they are sold at Asian grocery stores, outdoor stores, many supermarkets and online. They burn cleanly and quietly. Their weakness is cold weather, covered in the next section. Use the canister type your stove's maker specifies, and never use a damaged or dented canister.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Threaded Isobutane-Propane Canisters</h3>

          <p>
            Backpacking stoves like the MSR PocketRocket 2 and Jetboil Flash use threaded canisters of an isobutane and propane blend. They self-seal when removed and perform better in the cold than straight butane. They are sold mainly at outdoor and sporting goods stores, so they are the hardest of the three to find in a hurry after a storm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adapter Hoses and Larger Tanks</h3>

          <p>
            Many two-burner propane stoves can run from a 20 lb barbecue tank through an adapter hose, which is the cheapest way to cook through a long outage. Only use an adapter and regulator setup the stove's maker approves, check every connection for leaks with soapy water, and keep the tank outdoors and upright. If you are not sure whether your stove supports it, check the manual before buying parts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold-weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Butane in Cold Weather: Why It Fades Below Freezing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Winter is when power outages hurt most, and it is also when butane stoves are weakest. Butane boils at roughly the freezing point of water. Below that, the liquid in the canister struggles to turn into gas, pressure drops and the flame shrinks or sputters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Happens on a Cold Morning</h3>

          <p>
            A butane stove that roared in summer may barely heat a pot at 25 degrees Fahrenheit. As the canister cools from use, output can fall further. Many butane stoves also have pressure safety features that behave differently at low pressure, so performance is less predictable in the cold.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Work Around It</h3>

          <p>
            Keep butane canisters indoors at room temperature until you cook, and bring them out just before use. Never heat a canister with a flame, a heater or hot water, and never leave one in a hot car or near the stove's burner. If you live where outages happen in freezing weather, choose a propane stove, or the Gas One GS-3900PB, which can switch to a 1 lb propane cylinder.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane Is the Cold-Weather Fuel</h3>

          <p>
            Propane stays a gas at temperatures far colder than butane, which is why two-burner propane stoves are the safer bet for winter outage cooking. Isobutane blends sit in between, which is why backpackers use them in shoulder seasons.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wind" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Wind Performance: The Windscreen Matters More Than BTU
          </h2>

          <p>
            Outdoors, wind is the biggest thief of cooking heat. A light breeze can bend a flame away from the bottom of the pot and double your boil time, and a gusty day can blow out a weak burner entirely. Since outage cooking happens outside, often in storm weather, wind protection is a real buying factor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Built-In Wind Guards</h3>

          <p>
            Two-burner propane stoves use the lid and folding side panels as a windscreen. The Camp Chef Everest 2X lists a three-sided windscreen and the Coleman Triton+ lists dual wind guards. Butane stoves rely on a ring or baffle around the burner: the Iwatani 35FW lists a double windbreaker and the Gas One GS-3900PB a windblocker on all four corners of the burner head.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Integrated Systems Resist Wind Best</h3>

          <p>
            Integrated canister systems like the Jetboil Flash enclose the flame under a pot that locks to the burner, so wind has much less flame to push around. Open backpacking burners like the PocketRocket 2 need shelter from a pack, rocks or a canister-safe windscreen.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Set Up Smart</h3>

          <p>
            Put the open side of the stove away from the wind and use a wall, vehicle or table edge as a break, but never box a stove in so tightly that heat builds up around the fuel cylinder or canister. Keep the stove well away from walls you would otherwise use for shelter if they are flammable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="simmer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Simmer Control: The Spec Listings Rarely Prove
          </h2>

          <p>
            Boiling water is easy for almost any stove. Simmering a sauce without scorching it is where cheap stoves fall down. A good simmer means the burner can turn down low and hold steady without going out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Listings Claim</h3>

          <p>
            Coleman lists independently adjustable burners with PerfectFlow and PerfectHeat technology for precise temperature on the Triton+, and its standard Triton listing names simmering control. MSR says the PocketRocket 2 adjusts from a simmer to a rolling boil. Jetboil says the MiniMo's regulator goes from a light simmer to full boil. The Jetboil Flash makes no simmer claim because it is designed for boiling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Get a Better Simmer</h3>

          <p>
            Use a heavier pan to spread heat, keep the stove sheltered from wind so a low flame does not blow out, and on two-burner stoves turn the burner up slightly before turning it down again if it falters. A flame tamer or heat diffuser plate can help on stoves that run hot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When It Matters</h3>

          <p>
            If you mostly boil water, simmer control does not matter. If you cook rice, sauces or eggs, choose a stove that names simmer control in its listing and test it at home before you need it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel-planning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Much Fuel to Store for a Power Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running out of fuel on day three of an outage is a common mistake. A little arithmetic beforehand avoids it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start From the Listed Burn Time</h3>

          <p>
            Coleman lists up to 1 hour on high per 16 oz propane cylinder for the Triton+, and up to 1.25 hours on high per 8.8 oz butane cylinder for its Classic 1-burner. Real cooking runs mostly below high, so a cylinder usually lasts longer, but plan from the listed figure to build in a margin.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Simple Week Estimate</h3>

          <p>
            If you cook two meals a day and boil water for coffee, assume roughly 45 to 60 minutes of burner time per day. On a stove that burns a cylinder in about an hour on high, that is roughly one cylinder per day, so seven cylinders for a week, plus one spare. On a 20 lb tank through an approved adapter, a week is usually comfortable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Store Fuel Safely</h3>

          <p>
            Store propane cylinders outdoors or in a detached, ventilated shed, upright and away from heat and flames. Keep butane canisters in a cool, dry place away from heat sources, out of direct sun and away from children. Follow the cylinder labels, the stove's manual and any local fire codes on how much fuel you can store at home.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage-kit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. A Camping Stove Is One Part of an Outage Kit
          </h2>

          <p>
            A stove solves hot food and hot water. The rest of an outage plan covers power, light, heat and safety.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power</h3>

          <p>
            A portable power station or a generator keeps a refrigerator, phones and medical devices running. Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generator for power outages</a> guide covers portable, inverter and standby generators, and our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">best portable power station for camping</a> guide covers quiet battery stations that run indoors without fumes. For phones, a <a href="/best-camping-power-bank/">camping power bank</a> is the cheapest backup.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Light</h3>

          <p>
            You will cook outside after dark more often than you expect in winter. A lantern on the table and a headlamp on your head make outdoor cooking far safer than holding a phone light.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat</h3>

          <p>
            Never use a stove for heat. Use a heater rated for indoor use, with an oxygen depletion sensor, and keep a CO alarm running.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="car-vs-backpacking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Car Camping vs Backpacking: Weight, Pots and People
          </h2>

          <p>
            The camping half of the decision comes down to how far you carry the stove and how many people you feed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Car Camping and Tailgating</h3>

          <p>
            If the stove rides in a car, weight does not matter. Buy a two-burner propane stove with a good windscreen, and cook real meals in full-size pans. The Triton+ lists room for a 12-inch and a 10-inch pan, and the Everest 2X lists a 215 square inch cooking area that fits two pans.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Backpacking</h3>

          <p>
            On foot, every ounce counts. A 2.6 oz PocketRocket 2 with a small pot, or an integrated Jetboil for boiling water, is the norm. Plan meals around boiling water, and carry a lighter even if the stove has an igniter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Groups and Families</h3>

          <p>
            For four or more people, one burner is a bottleneck. Either buy a two-burner, or pair a butane single burner with a backpacking stove so two pots can cook at once.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Setting Up, Leak-Checking and Storing a Camp Stove
          </h2>

          <p>
            A camp stove that sat in the garage for two years needs a check before the next storm, not during it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leak-Check Every Connection</h3>

          <p>
            Brush soapy water on the connections between cylinder, regulator and hose. Bubbles mean a leak; tighten or replace the part and test again. Never check for leaks with a flame.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean Burners and Grates</h3>

          <p>
            Grease and spills clog burner ports and cause uneven flames. Lift out the grates, wipe the cooktop and clear the burner ports gently, following the maker's instructions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Store Without Fuel Attached</h3>

          <p>
            Remove the propane cylinder or butane canister before storing the stove, cap cylinders and keep the stove and fuel in a cool, dry place. Do a test burn outdoors at the start of every storm season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cooking Indoors Because It Is Cold Outside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A winter outage tempts people to move the camp stove into the kitchen. Do not. Camp stoves make carbon monoxide and have no vent. Cook outdoors in an open area and keep a battery CO alarm working inside.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Stove as a Heater</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A camp stove is not a space heater. Running it for warmth in a closed room is one of the classic causes of carbon monoxide poisoning after storms. Use a heater rated for indoor use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Butane for a Freezing Climate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Butane loses pressure near and below freezing, so a butane-only stove may barely work during a winter outage. Choose propane, or a dual fuel stove that can switch to a 1 lb propane cylinder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stocking Two Cylinders for a Week</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                One 16 oz cylinder may last about an hour on high. Work out fuel from the listed burn time and stock enough for every day you might be without power, plus a spare.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Testing the Stove Before the Storm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Igniters fail, hoses crack and burner ports clog in storage. Leak-check and do a test burn outdoors at the start of every storm season, and keep a lighter with the stove.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best camping stove?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most households, the Camp Chef Everest 2X is the best camping stove: two 20,000 BTU burners, matchless ignition and a three-sided windscreen, per its listing, which make it strong for family meals and windy conditions. The Coleman Triton+ is the value two-burner, and the Iwatani 35FW is the best single butane burner.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a camping stove indoors during a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Propane and butane camp stoves burn fuel and make carbon monoxide, and they have no vent. In a closed-up house during an outage that is dangerous. Cook outdoors in an open, ventilated area away from doors and windows, and keep a battery carbon monoxide alarm working inside. Some butane stoves are sold for indoor use; follow their manual and ventilate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best stove for a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A two-burner propane camp stove used outside, such as the Camp Chef Everest 2X or Coleman Triton+, because propane is easy to find and works in cold weather. If you are unsure which fuel stores will have, the Gas One GS-3900PB runs on butane or 1 lb propane. Always cook outdoors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is butane or propane better for a camping stove?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Propane is better in cold weather and is the most widely sold camp fuel in North America. Butane canisters are cheaper, slimmer and burn quietly, but butane struggles near and below freezing. For winter outages, choose propane. For mild weather and tabletop cooking, a butane stove is a good, compact choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a 1 lb propane cylinder last on a camp stove?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the stove and the flame. Coleman lists up to 1 hour on high for the Triton+ on one 16 oz cylinder. Cooking at medium heat stretches that. For outage planning, budget about one cylinder per day of normal cooking and keep a spare, or use a maker-approved adapter to a larger tank.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a butane stove in cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only with difficulty. Butane boils at around the freezing point of water, so in freezing weather canister pressure drops and the flame weakens. Keep canisters at room temperature until you cook, never heat them, and for freezing conditions use a propane stove or a dual fuel model that can switch to propane.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a Jetboil good for emergencies?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For boiling water, yes. Jetboil says the Flash boils 16 oz of water in 2 minutes, and the stove and a small canister pack into its own cup, which makes it a good go-bag stove. It is not made for frying or simmering, and it serves one or two people, so a household should pair it with a larger stove.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

